/E (The passage of the 18th Amendment in 1920, which prohibited the production, sale, and transportation of alcoholic beverages in the United States, transformed the landscape of organized crime almost immediately. What had previously been a collection of smaller, localized criminal activities expanded into a highly profitable and large-scale underground economy. Alcohol did not disappear with Prohibition; instead, demand remained extremely high, creating a black market that required organization, protection, and distribution on a level that had not existed before.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [3]
